      <h2 class="text-center section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-section">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What height fence do I need for my court?</h3>
          <p>Most residential courts use 10-foot fencing for effective ball containment. Tennis courts may benefit from 12-foot heights, while pickleball courts can often use 8-10 feet. We assess your specific needs based on court size, usage, and local requirements.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Can I add fencing to an existing court?</h3>
          <p>Absolutely! We frequently add fencing to existing courts that were built without enclosures. We carefully assess the site conditions and design fencing that complements your existing court while providing the containment and security you need.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Do I need permits for court fencing?</h3>
          <p>Most communities require permits for fencing over 6 feet in height. We help navigate the permit process and provide professional drawings and specifications needed for approval. HOA approval may also be required in some areas.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>How long does fence installation take?</h3>
          <p>Most residential court fencing is completed in 3-5 business days, depending on fence height, gates, and accessories. Weather conditions and site access can affect timing, but we work efficiently to minimize disruption.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What's the difference between galvanized and vinyl-coated fencing?</h3>
          <p>Galvanized fencing offers excellent durability at lower cost with a traditional silver appearance. Vinyl-coated fencing provides superior weather resistance, color options, and a more professional appearance at slightly higher cost.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

